    {
      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To solve this problem, we need to follow a systematic approach. Let's break down the problem step by step.\n\n1. **Identify the groups of books:**\n   We have two groups of books: one group of 3 math books and another group of 5 English books.\n\n2. **Consider the placement of the two groups:**\n   Since the two groups must stay together, we can treat each group as a single \"super book.\" \n   \n   - There are 2 ways to arrange which group goes on the left and which group goes on the right. Each arrangement corresponds to a specific combination of math books and English books near each other.\n\n   \\[\n   \\text{Number of ways to arrange groups} = 2\n   \\]\n\n3. **Arrange the books within the groups:**\n   - There are \\(3!\\) ways to arrange 3 different math books.\n     \\[\n     3! = 3 \\times 2 \\times 1 = 6\n     \\]\n\n   - There are \\(5!\\) ways to arrange 5 different English books.\n     \\[\n     5! = 5 \\times 4 \\times 3 \\times 2 \\times 1 = 120\n     \\]\n\n4. **Calculate the total number of arrangements:**\n   - The total number of ways to arrange both groups is the product of the number of ways to arrange each group and the number of ways to arrange the books within each group.\n   \\[\n   \\text{Total arrangements} = (\\text{Arrangements of groups}) \\times (\\text{Arrangements of math books within groups}) \\times (\\text{Arrangements of English books within groups})\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\text{Total arrangements} = 2 \\times 6 \\times 120\n   \\]\n\n5. **Final calculation:**\n   \\[\n   2 \\times 6 \\times 120 = 1440\n   \\]\n\nThus, the number of ways to arrange the books is \\(\\boxed{1440}\\).",
