      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "1. Since all the math books must stay together and all the English books must also stay together, we can treat them as separate units.\n2. We have 2 units to arrange: the math books unit and the English books unit.\n3. There are 2 ways to arrange these 2 units: math book unit 1 can be placed before the English books unit 1 or vice versa.\n4. We can now treat the math books unit as a single entity within the arrangement, so the math books unit can also be arranged within itself in 3 ways (3!) as they are all different.\n5. Similarly, we can treat the English books unit as another single entity within the arrangement, so the 5 English books can be arranged within themselves in 5! = 120 ways.\n6. To account for overcounting, we need to divide by the total number of books and the number of ways to arrange the books within their units to avoid double-counting.\n7. So, the total number of arrangements is (2)(3!) * (120) / (8 * 56) = 0.\n\nTherefore, there is 0 ways to put the math books and English books on a shelf with all the math books together and all the English books together.\nThe answer is: 0",
